{
  "dom_id": "step-q122004",
  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14217745/assets/9c5d2e2426a817b62f8cd834d7d31786c5f2dfd52a7b784fd0bf337abed6c4fc.png)\n\nConsider the region bounded by $y={e}^{2-x}$ and $y={e}^{{x}^{2}},$ as shown above. Which of the following gives the volume of the solid generated when that region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis?",
  "worked_solution": "Let $f(x)={e}^{2-x}$ and $g(x)={e}^{{x}^{2}}.$\n\nFirst, we determine the limits of integration. To do that, we find the intersections of the curves by solving the following equation for $x:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =g(x) \\\\ {e}^{2-x} & ={e}^{{x}^{2}} \\\\ 2-x & ={x}^{2} \\\\ 0 & ={x}^{2}+x-2 \\\\ (x+2)(x-1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the limits of integration are from $x=-2$ to $x=1.$\n\nLet's draw our vertical arrow through the region to determine the inner function and the outer function. The arrow should be pointing *away* from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14217745/assets/8a7f49165698bdac791a31a058c3228e6e2beab9f5ee9393971aa0e9aecd27b8.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner function $y=g(x)={e}^{{x}^{2}},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer function $y=f(x)={e}^{2-x}.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{a}^{b}([\\text{outer}]^{2}-[\\text{inner}]^{2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{1}({[{e}^{2-x}]}^{2}-{[{e}^{{x}^{2}}]}^{2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{1}({e}^{4-2x}-{e}^{2{x}^{2}})\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
